window.docContents[248].resolve({"/Multiplicity-one-_LPAR_the-Main-Lemma_RPAR_/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--Multiplicity-one-_LPAR_the-Main-Lemma_RPAR_":{"contents":"(Main Lemma — Miyake Theorem 4.6.8.) A cusp form in  whose Fourier\ncoefficients  vanish for all  coprime to  lies in the old subspace\n (, ). This\nis the engine of the whole theory: it converts \"almost all coefficients vanish\" into a\nstructural conclusion.\n\nThis is the per-character form of Miyake Theorem 4.6.8. Vanishing of all coprime\ncoefficients lets one run the squarefree descent (peel off one prime at a time via the\n/ degeneracy maps and the conductor dichotomy 4.6.4), expressing the form as a\nsame-level sum of degeneracy images of lower-level forms — i.e. an oldform.\n\nA newform of level  and weight  is an eigenform lying in the new\nsubspace and normalised so that its first Fourier coefficient is \n(, ). In LeanModularForms this is the\nstructure , extending  with the new-membership and\nnormalisation fields. Following Miyake §4.6.\n\nA nonzero newform at level  does not lie in the (extended) old subspace\n(, ). Its eigenvalue system is genuinely of\nlevel , occurring at no proper divisor.\n\nA newform lies in the new subspace, which meets the old subspace only in \n(); a nonzero new form therefore cannot be old.\n\n","context":"Strong Multiplicity One — Lean blueprint","header":"5. Multiplicity one (the Main Lemma)","id":"/Multiplicity-one-_LPAR_the-Main-Lemma_RPAR_/#Strong-Multiplicity-One-___-Lean-blueprint--Multiplicity-one-_LPAR_the-Main-Lemma_RPAR_"}});